      "401 and 403 handling exists, but it is implemented as navigator callback side effects and not demonstrated through real Next.js routing or browser navigation.",
      "The acceptance item requiring browser smoke for a valid and invalid upload path is not actually met by the emitted test package."
    ]
  },
  "source_substance": {
    "status": "adequate",
    "notes": [
      "The candidate has meaningful implementation mass for a narrow frontend REQ: shared API client, runtime/auth wiring, reusable dropzone, mutation hook, page component, and route entrypoint.",
      "Validation logic, error translation, success rendering, and runtime-driven next action construction are real logic, not placeholders.",
      "The code remains intentionally narrow and depends on a host app shell for QueryClientProvider and route integration, so it is not standalone promotable yet.",
      "Desktop-first behavior is expressed mainly through layout/styling classes; that is acceptable for this slice but not deeply proven."
    ]
  },
  "test_substance": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Tests cover valid upload via drag-and-drop, invalid format rejection, oversize rejection, and 401/403 error branches.",
      "The tests exercise real component behavior with React Testing Library and TanStack Query rather than only snapshotting structure.",
      "The package does not include true browser smoke or e2e automation, despite the REQ acceptance and PLAN kit-readiness expecting that level of evidence.",
      "Redirect assertions are limited to mocked navigator calls and status text; they do not prove integration with actual route transitions.",
      "No test proves a successful file-picker happy path, only drag-and-drop success and file-picker negatives."
    ]
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC all point to the correct emitted files and the documented commands align with the actual test artifact.",
      "The documentation accurately states that the slice depends on upstream shell/provider wiring and on REQ-002 and REQ-003.",
      "The docs also claim the jsdom tests are sufficient for REQ-004 acceptance and describe them as smoke coverage; that is stronger than the actual evidence.",
      "LTC maps acceptance to tests, but one acceptance item is only approximated, not satisfied, because no real browser smoke is emitted."
    ]
  },
  "promotion_fit": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Module placement follows the PLAN-defined frontend namespaces and shared families, which is good promotion discipline.",
      "The implementation is localized and reviewable, making later promotion into canonical src/tests targets feasible.",
      "Promotion is not yet low-risk enough to treat as hardening-ready because the navigation proof and browser-level acceptance evidence are missing.",
      "The current candidate is better than a stub, but it still behaves like a staging slice that assumes host shell integration rather than proving it."
    ]
  },
  "shared_common_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "Use of shared frontend modules is justified: API client, auth/runtime wiring, and dropzone UI are reasonable reusable seams for adjacent slices.",
      "The candidate stays within the shared module families authorized by PLAN and does not invent parallel roots.",
      "There is no obvious premature or excessive common extraction in this slice."
    ]
  },
  "dual_mode_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "Local and aws modes preserve the same business flow and differ mainly through environment-driven configuration.",
      "The same page, hook, and API contract are used in both modes, which is the correct promotable pattern.",
      "Target-runtime claims are lightly evidenced only through env substitution; they are not validated against real deployment/runtime wiring."
    ]
  },
  "blockers": [
    "Required browser smoke coverage for one valid and one invalid upload path is missing; only jsdom component/integration tests are emitted.",
    "401/403 acceptance is only simulated through injected navigator callbacks and status messaging, not proven through real routing/navigation behavior.",
    "Docs and LTC overstate acceptance completeness by treating jsdom tests as sufficient browser smoke evidence."
  ],
  "hardening_targets": [
    "Add actual browser or e2e smoke for one successful upload and one invalid upload path, consistent with REQ-004 acceptance and PLAN expectations.",
    "Prove 401 and 403 route handling through the real navigation layer used after promotion, not only mocked auth navigator callbacks.",
    "Tighten README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC language so test claims match the actual evidence and do not overclaim acceptance closure.",
    "Add at least one promotion-facing integration test that exercises the route entrypoint with required provider wiring instead of testing only the inner page component."
  ]
